# Saving a project to GitHub

## Every time (project already has a repo)

cd ~/Projects/Tetris-cpp
git status
git add main.cpp assets
git commit -m “short description of what changed”
git push


## What each command does
- `git status` = what changed since the last commit
- `git add <files>` = pick which files go into the next commit
- `git commit -m "..."` = save a snapshot locally, with a message
- `git push` = upload the commits to GitHub

## Rules for green squares
- commit must be on the default branch (main)
- `git config user.email` must match an email on my GitHub account
- small commits, one per finished thing

## Ignoring files
- compiled binary doesn't belong in the repo
- `echo "tetris" >> .gitignore`

## First time with a new project

cd ~/Projects/my-project
git init
git add .
git commit -m “first commit”
git branch -M main
git remote add origin git@github.com:KairoTheGoat/my-project.git
git push -u origin main


## Dotfiles repo
- my repo lives in `~/rice`
- copy the config in, then add, commit, push:
  `cp /etc/sddm.conf ~/rice/sddm/`

## If something breaks
- `git remote -v` = where does this repo push to
- `git log --oneline` = list of commits
- push rejected = run `git pull` first, then push again
